WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.121 --> 00:00:03.430
(hip hop instrumental)

00:00:03.430 --> 00:00:04.630
- What is up everybody?

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:06.110
I've been reading the comments

00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:08.350
and I notice a trend
amongst those comments.

00:00:08.350 --> 00:00:11.510
A lot of you wanna know what
my pre-med journey was like.

00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:14.050
I'm incredibly happy that
you wanna know my story

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:16.270
and I will be telling you everything

00:00:16.270 --> 00:00:18.940
from why I went into medicine,

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:20.530
what college was like,

00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:22.110
what med school was like,

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:23.799
and give you some tips along the way.

00:00:23.799 --> 00:00:25.470
(hip hop instrumental)

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:28.240
So right now I'm 27 years old

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:31.440
I'm fresh out of residency
a couple of months out

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.930
but I think a good place to start

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:36.870
would probably be
somewhere in high school.

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:38.900
Maybe sophomore year of high school.

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:41.510
Because I remember it was at that point

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:43.318
I was pretty firm that I wanted

00:00:43.318 --> 00:00:45.560
to go into the field of medicine.

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:47.390
And I'll tell you how I got there.

00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:50.840
My dad is a doctor, he was
a doctor back in Russia

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:52.400
before we even came to the states

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:54.210
and when we came to the U.S.,

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:56.230
he decided to go through medical school

00:00:56.230 --> 00:00:57.960
for the second time in his life.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:00.130
And this is in a new language,

00:01:00.130 --> 00:01:02.700
he didn't speak English, in his 40's.

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:04.730
I had a very unique perspective

00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:07.300
watching my dad go through medical school.

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:10.250
Take all the classes
for the first two years,

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:12.750
then go on his clinical
rotation the next two years,

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:15.170
and then going into his residency.

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:17.390
I mean when it was take
your kid to work day

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:18.670
he brought me to the hospital

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:19.720
in his residency.

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:20.930
And that's the good part about it.

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:23.430
The bad was that I wasn't able to complain

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:24.320
and if I ever did,

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:26.407
he's like "Shut up, because I did it

00:01:26.407 --> 00:01:28.360
"in much harder circumstances."

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:29.740
and with that, I will not argue.

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:33.010
Let me tell you how I went into medicine,

00:01:33.010 --> 00:01:35.470
or how I decided my heart was in medicine.

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:38.780
First of all when I was in
high school I loved science.

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:40.270
That was my number one class,

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:42.780
I performed best in that class

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:45.550
and I was always curious
about the human body.

00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:49.290
I wanted to know why
certain things happened.

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:51.450
Why your stomach hurt, why your head hurt,

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:54.370
but that's not what made me
decide to go into medicine.

00:01:54.370 --> 00:01:56.020
When I saw my dad with his patients,

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:57.460
and it happened quite frequently,

00:01:57.460 --> 00:02:01.583
I realized, medicine is
a field you can go into

00:02:01.583 --> 00:02:05.600
and not have a responsibility to a client,

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:09.390
to a shareholder, to a board member,

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:12.530
but it was to the person sitting
directly in front of you.

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:15.350
And I started to look into the
different options there are

00:02:15.350 --> 00:02:17.530
to go into the field of medicine.

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:21.240
You have M.D. schools,
you have D.O. schools,

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:23.450
you have Caribbean M.D. schools.

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:25.970
But after spending some
time watching my dad

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:27.260
go through D.O. school

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:29.100
and doing some of the manipulations

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:31.800
and the hands on OMT
things that we do as D.O.s,

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:34.740
on me after injuries in
TaeKwonDo and soccer,

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:36.780
and seeing phenomenal results,

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:38.690
I said "I wanna be a D.O."

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:40.490
and I became really passionate

00:02:40.490 --> 00:02:43.720
in finding a route that
will get me to be a doctor

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:45.430
in the fastest way possible.

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:47.890
In high school, I'm not gonna lie,

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:49.640
I wasn't the studious type,

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:51.233
I didn't spend hours studying,

00:02:51.233 --> 00:02:53.740
a lot of the information
pretty much came to me.

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:55.760
I had a really good memory because

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:58.500
when I was in middle school

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:00.740
my dad actually, when he
came home from med school

00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:01.700
and he was exhausted,

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:04.020
he would give me my
social studies textbook,

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:06.860
ask me what my required
reading for that night was,

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:10.680
and then tell me that I
have to memorize each page

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:11.930
while he holds the book,

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:15.160
and I have to rehearse and
tell him exactly word for word

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:16.070
what's on that page.

00:03:16.070 --> 00:03:18.100
To some people in society
they might see that

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:19.027
as something horrible.

00:03:19.027 --> 00:03:20.847
"Oh you're forcing your child to do this

00:03:20.847 --> 00:03:22.440
"when they don't want to."

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:24.300
But he was very disciplined that way

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:25.387
that he said "You wanna have fun?

00:03:25.387 --> 00:03:26.510
"You have to do this first."

00:03:26.510 --> 00:03:27.850
I learned how to memorize,

00:03:27.850 --> 00:03:30.590
I almost got a photographic
memory at one point

00:03:30.590 --> 00:03:32.280
when I would read these pages

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:33.190
and I would have to tell them back

00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:35.070
I could imagine the page in front of me

00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:36.990
and doing that practice in middle school

00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:39.030
really set me up for
success in high school

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:40.940
I decided in my junior year

00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:45.890
to start applying to
combined B.S./D.O. programs.

00:03:45.890 --> 00:03:47.740
B.S. is Bachelor's in Science,

00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:50.240
and D.O. is Doctor of
Osteopathic Medicine.

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:52.500
And it's basically a combined program,

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:54.090
an accelerated program,

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:56.450
where you do three years
of undergraduate studies

00:03:56.450 --> 00:03:58.250
and four years of medical school.

00:03:58.250 --> 00:04:00.150
And all you have to do is maintain

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:02.630
a pretty significant GPA,

00:04:02.630 --> 00:04:04.220
I believe it was like 3.5,

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:08.440
and then get a sort of,
a mediocre MCAT score,

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:09.273
and continue.

00:04:09.273 --> 00:04:11.310
I believe it was like
27 or 28 at the time.

00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:13.770
And I remember I applied early decision

00:04:13.770 --> 00:04:16.385
to NYIT's B.S./D.O. program.

00:04:16.385 --> 00:04:19.330
NYIT being New York
Institute of Technology,

00:04:19.330 --> 00:04:22.680
it's in Old Westbury in
Long Island in New York.

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:24.710
I believe they only
accepted like 90 applicants

00:04:24.710 --> 00:04:25.543
or something around there,

00:04:25.543 --> 00:04:26.963
so it's very competitive.

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:29.940
I did fairly well on my SAT,

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:32.250
I didn't get like a 1600
or anything like that,

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:33.600
but it was like mid-1400's.

00:04:35.030 --> 00:04:37.290
So you know, I did fairly well on that.

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:38.810
My GPA was pretty solid,

00:04:38.810 --> 00:04:40.340
and I was lucky enough to get into

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:42.100
the NYIT B.S./D.O. program.

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:43.066
So college.

00:04:43.066 --> 00:04:44.590
(hip hop instrumental)

00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:46.397
I took all the necessary classes,

00:04:46.397 --> 00:04:50.093
I had to maintain a very high GPA of 3.5,

00:04:51.230 --> 00:04:52.620
couldn't let it drop below that

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:55.520
and that was obviously
tough because every class,

00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:59.296
your grade was very
important to your future.

00:04:59.296 --> 00:05:02.530
It wasn't just "oh if
I get a B+ I'm fine."

00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:04.210
like a B was not good!

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:07.610
A B was a 3.0, so if you got a B,

00:05:07.610 --> 00:05:09.840
you were worried how
that affected your GPA.

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:12.936
I'm finishing up my third year,

00:05:12.936 --> 00:05:16.200
which is my last year of
undergraduate studies,

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:18.203
and I have to go in to take my MCAT.

00:05:19.250 --> 00:05:22.253
This is an incredibly
stressful time for everybody.

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:27.220
Some people didn't prep
enough for the MCAT,

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:32.220
some people decided that
medical school wasn't for them,

00:05:32.450 --> 00:05:34.670
and my program suddenly shrunk from

00:05:34.670 --> 00:05:37.485
like 90 or 88 or whatever
it was that it started with,

00:05:37.485 --> 00:05:38.800
to like 20.

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:40.860
But there are certain things you can do

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:42.130
to succeed with your MCAT.

00:05:42.130 --> 00:05:44.400
Number one, it's a predictable test.

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:46.877
There are Kaplan courses,
there are free courses online,

00:05:46.877 --> 00:05:49.533
there are boot camps that
you can go to and study

00:05:49.533 --> 00:05:51.330
for a couple of weeks at a time.

00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:53.088
And I know some of these things cost money

00:05:53.088 --> 00:05:56.130
and maybe you don't need the
boot camp that costs the most,

00:05:56.130 --> 00:05:59.150
but you can sign up for a Kaplan
class and put in the time.

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:01.237
To hear people say "I
can't do well in this test

00:06:01.237 --> 00:06:02.270
"no matter what I do."

00:06:02.270 --> 00:06:06.400
If you find a tutor, if you
find an online study program

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:09.730
there is no reason why you
shouldn't get the score

00:06:09.730 --> 00:06:12.270
that you need to, to get
into a medical school.

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:15.080
People say "Well how do I know
if medicine is right for me?"

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:16.583
That's a very common question.

00:06:18.190 --> 00:06:20.390
And it's a question that you
need to answer for yourself.

00:06:20.390 --> 00:06:21.950
I didn't know if medicine was right for me

00:06:21.950 --> 00:06:23.160
so I watched my dad do it.

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:25.630
I experienced it, I worked
in a medical setting,

00:06:25.630 --> 00:06:27.060
I knew what it felt like,

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:28.396
at least a rough concept,

00:06:28.396 --> 00:06:30.590
of what it's like to work as a doctor.

00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:34.070
Now there are some of you,
who's parents are doctors.

00:06:34.070 --> 00:06:36.070
There are some of you have
heard from your friends

00:06:36.070 --> 00:06:38.280
that being a doctor gets you rich.

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:41.060
These are not good reasons
to go into medicine.

00:06:41.060 --> 00:06:42.860
Now I'm not saying that to discourage you,

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:46.810
I'm just saying medicine
is a long term commitment.

00:06:46.810 --> 00:06:48.380
Residency hours are brutal.

00:06:48.380 --> 00:06:49.889
Med school is a lot of studying.

00:06:49.889 --> 00:06:53.240
Undergrad pre-med studying
is a lot of studying.

00:06:53.240 --> 00:06:55.807
And if you don't wanna be
there for the right reasons,

00:06:55.807 --> 00:06:59.137
"My dad's a doctor, my mom's a
doctor, my sister's a doctor,

00:06:59.137 --> 00:07:01.960
"Grey's Anatomy looks
awesome, Dr. Oz looks cool."

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:03.458
Good reasons to go into medicine:

00:07:03.458 --> 00:07:05.754
You can't see yourself
doing anything else,

00:07:05.754 --> 00:07:09.890
you have an insatiable curiosity
for the field of medicine,

00:07:09.890 --> 00:07:11.810
you wanna make a difference in the world,

00:07:11.810 --> 00:07:13.970
and science falls within your skillset.

00:07:13.970 --> 00:07:15.520
Those are good reasons.

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:17.370
A big thing I have to tell you about.

00:07:18.540 --> 00:07:20.410
This maybe applies more to medical school,

00:07:20.410 --> 00:07:23.210
but I think it applies to college as well.

00:07:23.210 --> 00:07:26.430
There will always be another test,

00:07:26.430 --> 00:07:28.580
there will always be another exam,

00:07:28.580 --> 00:07:31.295
there will always be something
else you're preparing for.

00:07:31.295 --> 00:07:36.295
If you start to stress out
about every single test,

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:38.560
you won't make it through the process.

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:41.050
Or you'll make it through the
process and be really unhappy,

00:07:41.050 --> 00:07:41.990
Or you'll make it through the process

00:07:41.990 --> 00:07:43.410
and be really unhealthy,

00:07:43.410 --> 00:07:46.570
because stress really sucks long term.

00:07:46.570 --> 00:07:48.230
So you shouldn't expose yourself to that.

00:07:48.230 --> 00:07:50.150
If you as an undergraduate student

00:07:50.150 --> 00:07:51.830
who's looking to go into medicine

00:07:51.830 --> 00:07:54.730
start worrying about the
test you have next Thursday,

00:07:54.730 --> 00:07:56.270
and then start thinking
about the test you have

00:07:56.270 --> 00:07:57.250
the following month,

00:07:57.250 --> 00:07:58.460
and then the following month,

00:07:58.460 --> 00:08:00.510
and then your MCATs, and then your boards,

00:08:00.510 --> 00:08:01.840
and then this and then that,

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:03.300
you'll go crazy.

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:06.630
Approach each test as an obstacle that's

00:08:06.630 --> 00:08:08.800
immediately in front of you
that you need to surpass.

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:11.830
Now, you've taken the MCAT,
you've gotten your scores,

00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:13.710
and it's time for you to do interviews.

00:08:13.710 --> 00:08:15.570
My journey through interviews was

00:08:15.570 --> 00:08:19.760
I had an interview in NYIT, in med school,

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:21.500
which is more of a formality.

00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:24.180
And yes, they ask you
some difficult questions,

00:08:24.180 --> 00:08:26.440
but again, as long as you
don't say anything too wild

00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:28.350
you're guaranteed a spot in the program

00:08:28.350 --> 00:08:29.770
because of the seven year contract

00:08:29.770 --> 00:08:32.400
that you went into in the program.

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:34.670
But, that doesn't mean I don't know

00:08:34.670 --> 00:08:37.160
what questions med
schools traditionally ask.

00:08:37.160 --> 00:08:39.910
All of that information
is available to you

00:08:39.910 --> 00:08:40.770
on the internet.

00:08:40.770 --> 00:08:42.940
Scour Student Doctor Network,

00:08:42.940 --> 00:08:45.070
look on different forums.

00:08:45.070 --> 00:08:46.445
If you're going into an interview,

00:08:46.445 --> 00:08:50.047
you have to know how
to answer the question

00:08:50.047 --> 00:08:51.700
"Why do you wanna be a doctor?"

00:08:51.700 --> 00:08:53.240
I mean you have to know.

00:08:53.240 --> 00:08:54.870
If you don't know the answer to that,

00:08:54.870 --> 00:08:56.869
or your answer's that "My dad's a doctor."

00:08:56.869 --> 00:09:00.330
I mean, that's awful.

00:09:00.330 --> 00:09:04.190
There's no way around it, you
have to know why you're there.

00:09:04.190 --> 00:09:06.710
And you have to be a person first.

00:09:06.710 --> 00:09:07.880
What does that mean?

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:10.420
That means, when I ask you a question,

00:09:10.420 --> 00:09:12.380
whether that's as an interviewer,

00:09:12.380 --> 00:09:15.880
I ask you a question like why
you wanna go into medicine,

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:17.720
or "Why this medical school?"

00:09:17.720 --> 00:09:20.650
I don't expect you to
have a brilliant answer,

00:09:20.650 --> 00:09:22.570
but I expect you to have
an answer that's based

00:09:22.570 --> 00:09:23.870
on your experiences,

00:09:23.870 --> 00:09:25.290
based on something that you did,

00:09:25.290 --> 00:09:27.110
based on something you felt,

00:09:27.110 --> 00:09:29.950
because if you tell me a generic answer,

00:09:29.950 --> 00:09:31.270
I'll know it's generic,

00:09:31.270 --> 00:09:33.030
because I hear those answers all the time.

00:09:33.030 --> 00:09:35.120
But if you tell me a unique answer,

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:36.930
that might not even blow my mind,

00:09:36.930 --> 00:09:39.090
but it's unique to you,

00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:41.810
I know I'm getting a human
applicant in front of me.

00:09:41.810 --> 00:09:44.040
Someone that can feel empathy,

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:46.010
who can verbalize what they're feeling.

00:09:46.010 --> 00:09:47.609
Be human, be appropriate,

00:09:47.609 --> 00:09:50.150
at times of sadness, be sad,

00:09:50.150 --> 00:09:52.370
when it's time to be angry, be angry.

00:09:52.370 --> 00:09:54.640
You know, experience all your emotions

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:57.840
don't feel like you
need to put on a front.

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:01.440
Am I saying don't be professional,
use profanity and slang?

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:02.640
No, be professional.

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:04.510
I'm saying be you,

00:10:04.510 --> 00:10:06.570
in a sense that you
should tell your story.

00:10:06.570 --> 00:10:08.480
It's not enough to just practice

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:12.600
having read questions and
practiced answers in your mind,

00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:14.340
You have to verbalize it.

00:10:14.340 --> 00:10:15.950
And that goes for public speaking too.

00:10:15.950 --> 00:10:17.300
Visualize a person in front of you

00:10:17.300 --> 00:10:19.410
asking you a question, and answer it.

00:10:19.410 --> 00:10:21.650
You'll stutter, you'll no doubt stutter.

00:10:21.650 --> 00:10:24.510
But that's a good thing,
get the stuttering out now,

00:10:24.510 --> 00:10:26.290
create your story so it flows.

00:10:26.290 --> 00:10:29.117
Once you verbalize and your
brain has heard you say it,

00:10:29.117 --> 00:10:31.260
it forms that pattern,

00:10:31.260 --> 00:10:33.576
those neurons link
together within your brain,

00:10:33.576 --> 00:10:35.590
and you will have a much easier time

00:10:35.590 --> 00:10:37.770
verbalizing all those things
at the time of the interview.

00:10:37.770 --> 00:10:39.420
(hip hop instrumental)

00:10:39.420 --> 00:10:42.430
Now it was time to get serious

00:10:42.430 --> 00:10:45.440
because noone's holding
your hand in medical school.

00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:48.670
If you start flunking, bye, you left.

00:10:48.670 --> 00:10:50.430
If you don't pass your boards, bye.

00:10:50.430 --> 00:10:54.080
I came into medical
school with the philosophy

00:10:54.080 --> 00:10:55.790
that I will overstudy,

00:10:55.790 --> 00:10:59.360
and then sort of decrease
the amount of time necessary

00:10:59.360 --> 00:11:00.870
to study for my exams,

00:11:00.870 --> 00:11:02.700
so I can find the best amount of time

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:05.823
that I need to dedicate
to do well on the exams

00:11:05.823 --> 00:11:08.277
but also to have a substantial life,

00:11:08.277 --> 00:11:11.010
a healthy life, outside
of medicine as well,

00:11:11.010 --> 00:11:12.480
because that's important.

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:14.840
You can't just bury your
head in a book for four years

00:11:14.840 --> 00:11:16.260
and forget society exists.

00:11:16.260 --> 00:11:18.460
There were a lot of people who were into

00:11:18.460 --> 00:11:21.170
what you would call like
performance enhancing pills,

00:11:21.170 --> 00:11:23.420
some people were taking caffeine pills

00:11:23.420 --> 00:11:26.490
to increase their ability to study longer,

00:11:26.490 --> 00:11:28.930
some people were taking
illegal things like

00:11:28.930 --> 00:11:30.470
Adderall without even a prescription

00:11:30.470 --> 00:11:31.680
they were buying it on the street.

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:33.920
I urge you, do not fall into this trap

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:35.970
of starting to take pharmaceuticals

00:11:35.970 --> 00:11:38.250
in order to improve your
performance in med school.

00:11:38.250 --> 00:11:39.740
It's gonna make you unhappy,

00:11:39.740 --> 00:11:41.780
you're gonna build a dependence on them,

00:11:41.780 --> 00:11:44.690
you're gonna change the
way you view your success,

00:11:44.690 --> 00:11:47.460
and you feel like it's
not rightfully earned.

00:11:47.460 --> 00:11:49.640
Don't do that, you will
regret your decision,

00:11:49.640 --> 00:11:50.770
I've seen people do it,

00:11:50.770 --> 00:11:52.210
seen people get sick from it,

00:11:52.210 --> 00:11:53.560
it's not a smart choice.

00:11:53.560 --> 00:11:56.060
So let me talk about my
med school experience,

00:11:56.060 --> 00:11:58.410
and a lot of medical schools
work in a similar fashion

00:11:58.410 --> 00:11:59.560
but not all.

00:11:59.560 --> 00:12:01.943
The first two years are
learning in the classroom,

00:12:01.943 --> 00:12:03.370
lecture based learning.

00:12:03.370 --> 00:12:06.560
And then the second two
years are clinical rotations

00:12:06.560 --> 00:12:08.090
that you spend in the hospital.

00:12:08.090 --> 00:12:10.650
And those hospitals are ones that carry

00:12:10.650 --> 00:12:12.160
an affiliation with your med school.

00:12:12.160 --> 00:12:15.890
NYIT luckily had a lot of
great clinical affiliation,

00:12:15.890 --> 00:12:17.080
so I was able to do rotations

00:12:17.080 --> 00:12:17.970
at a lot of sites.

00:12:17.970 --> 00:12:22.300
My school was great in the sense that

00:12:22.300 --> 00:12:24.200
it streamed all of the
lectures in addition

00:12:24.200 --> 00:12:25.660
to going and viewing the lecture.

00:12:25.660 --> 00:12:27.512
I wasn't a fan of studying the packets

00:12:27.512 --> 00:12:28.840
that they gave.

00:12:28.840 --> 00:12:33.270
So I watched the lectures
on accelerated speed.

00:12:33.270 --> 00:12:35.617
And a lot of people were like

00:12:35.617 --> 00:12:37.047
"Mike are you crazy I
don't even understand

00:12:37.047 --> 00:12:38.160
"what they're saying when you put it on

00:12:38.160 --> 00:12:40.280
"1.5 speed or two speed."

00:12:40.280 --> 00:12:41.810
But look it worked for me.

00:12:41.810 --> 00:12:44.290
I was able to watch the lectures

00:12:44.290 --> 00:12:46.470
two times over, in the
time that most people

00:12:46.470 --> 00:12:48.120
watched them in a single session.

00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:49.610
If you have a dream school, great.

00:12:49.610 --> 00:12:50.806
I'm happy that you're dreaming,

00:12:50.806 --> 00:12:52.888
I'm happy that you have a goal.

00:12:52.888 --> 00:12:55.860
But if you don't get into your number one,

00:12:55.860 --> 00:12:58.100
or even your number two
or number three school,

00:12:58.100 --> 00:13:00.070
it's not the end of the world.

00:13:00.070 --> 00:13:02.770
It really almost doesn't
make a difference.

00:13:02.770 --> 00:13:05.710
If you wanna learn, in this day and age,

00:13:05.710 --> 00:13:07.440
all the material's there for you.

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:09.810
Yes you should go to the
best school that you can

00:13:09.810 --> 00:13:11.430
for your circumstances.

00:13:11.430 --> 00:13:13.210
You know, what your abilities are,

00:13:13.210 --> 00:13:14.920
what your surroundings are like,

00:13:14.920 --> 00:13:17.000
where your physical location is,

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:20.240
unfortunately to say, how much
financial ability you have.

00:13:20.240 --> 00:13:21.700
But if you don't get
your number one school,

00:13:21.700 --> 00:13:22.890
don't panic.

00:13:22.890 --> 00:13:24.960
Work hard, work harder,

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:26.490
but don't think it's the end of the world,

00:13:26.490 --> 00:13:28.110
I promise you it's not.

00:13:28.110 --> 00:13:31.320
So you finish your first
two years of learning,

00:13:31.320 --> 00:13:33.760
and now comes my favorite part:

00:13:33.760 --> 00:13:36.020
Step 1 Boards.

00:13:36.020 --> 00:13:37.880
This a tough phase because not only

00:13:37.880 --> 00:13:39.680
are you studying for your current classes

00:13:39.680 --> 00:13:41.070
and trying to do well there,

00:13:41.070 --> 00:13:43.140
but you also have to study for your boards

00:13:43.140 --> 00:13:44.730
and your Step 1 exam is probably

00:13:44.730 --> 00:13:45.909
the most important exam

00:13:45.909 --> 00:13:48.920
that is gonna decide
which residency you get

00:13:48.920 --> 00:13:50.680
which residency you go into in the future.

00:13:50.680 --> 00:13:51.860
It's not the only thing that matters,

00:13:51.860 --> 00:13:53.920
but it's one of the most important things.

00:13:53.920 --> 00:13:55.510
So it's one of those tests,

00:13:55.510 --> 00:13:57.850
similar to the MCAT, that
people freak out about,

00:13:57.850 --> 00:13:59.360
and it's important to not do that,

00:13:59.360 --> 00:14:00.810
it's important to prepare.

00:14:00.810 --> 00:14:02.370
The more questions you do,

00:14:02.370 --> 00:14:04.220
the more preparations you get,

00:14:04.220 --> 00:14:05.640
the better you're gonna do on the test.

00:14:05.640 --> 00:14:06.760
That's the bottom line.

00:14:06.760 --> 00:14:08.140
Especially if you're already doing well

00:14:08.140 --> 00:14:09.280
in your current classes.

00:14:09.280 --> 00:14:10.760
The number one advice I can give you

00:14:10.760 --> 00:14:13.040
is do questions, questions, questions,

00:14:13.040 --> 00:14:16.850
because it's been proven
in scientific trials

00:14:16.850 --> 00:14:21.630
that recognition and fetching that idea

00:14:21.630 --> 00:14:23.600
from your mind, retrieving it,

00:14:23.600 --> 00:14:26.300
bringing it back to
paper in a question form,

00:14:26.300 --> 00:14:28.460
is the best way of memorizing material.

00:14:28.460 --> 00:14:31.430
So don't be afraid to
do two question banks

00:14:31.430 --> 00:14:32.263
even if you need to.

00:14:32.263 --> 00:14:34.580
In your third year you
start something known

00:14:34.580 --> 00:14:35.647
as clinical rotations.

00:14:35.647 --> 00:14:38.852
Where you'll get a schedule
of either one hospital

00:14:38.852 --> 00:14:40.480
or multiple hospitals,

00:14:40.480 --> 00:14:43.100
and different departments
that you can be rotating in.

00:14:43.100 --> 00:14:44.183
This was my favorite
part of medical school,

00:14:44.183 --> 00:14:46.060
because I love experiencing stuff,

00:14:46.060 --> 00:14:47.290
I love getting stuff done.

00:14:47.290 --> 00:14:49.360
My first rotation was internal medicine,

00:14:49.360 --> 00:14:51.240
where you're treating
hospitalized patients

00:14:51.240 --> 00:14:52.600
for all sorts of illnesses.

00:14:52.600 --> 00:14:54.610
I didn't come in pretending
I knew everything

00:14:54.610 --> 00:14:56.240
or I didn't come in with an attitude.

00:14:56.240 --> 00:14:58.053
I'm eager to learn, and what I don't know

00:14:58.053 --> 00:14:59.820
I promised I'll learn in the future.

00:14:59.820 --> 00:15:01.520
The residents really liked that about me,

00:15:01.520 --> 00:15:04.140
because when they needed help,

00:15:04.140 --> 00:15:07.320
I would volunteer my
services as much as I could

00:15:07.320 --> 00:15:08.760
without obviously being annoying

00:15:08.760 --> 00:15:10.130
or overstepping bounds

00:15:10.130 --> 00:15:15.130
or pushing other student's
learning after mine.

00:15:15.380 --> 00:15:16.950
I always try to make it fair.

00:15:16.950 --> 00:15:18.850
But any time that I
could help somebody out,

00:15:18.850 --> 00:15:20.850
stay late, do an extra procedure,

00:15:20.850 --> 00:15:23.080
I would do it so I could learn it.

00:15:23.080 --> 00:15:24.290
Or if I didn't know how to do it,

00:15:24.290 --> 00:15:26.200
I asked if I could watch someone do it.

00:15:26.200 --> 00:15:28.006
And that old mantra of

00:15:28.006 --> 00:15:32.870
"See one, do one, teach
one" really applies here.

00:15:32.870 --> 00:15:35.120
When I saw a resident do
it, I learned how to do it,

00:15:35.120 --> 00:15:36.860
then the resident would watch me do it,

00:15:36.860 --> 00:15:38.110
approve of how I did it,

00:15:38.110 --> 00:15:40.390
and next time I would teach
another student how to do it.

00:15:40.390 --> 00:15:42.593
And really that's the
ultimate way to learn

00:15:42.593 --> 00:15:45.290
by repeating that process
over and over again.

00:15:45.290 --> 00:15:46.540
Enjoy the process man,

00:15:46.540 --> 00:15:48.490
that's the thing I have to tell you.

00:15:48.490 --> 00:15:49.323
I would hear some people say

00:15:49.323 --> 00:15:50.257
"Ah I just wanna get out of here,

00:15:50.257 --> 00:15:51.343
"I wanna get out of here early."

00:15:51.343 --> 00:15:52.897
or "I don't wanna come in so early,

00:15:52.897 --> 00:15:54.880
"this rotation sucks."

00:15:54.880 --> 00:15:57.570
You chose this career,
you wanted to be here,

00:15:57.570 --> 00:15:58.870
this is what you wanna do,

00:15:58.870 --> 00:16:00.610
this is what you're gonna
be doing your whole life.

00:16:00.610 --> 00:16:03.040
How can you be complaining
about being here

00:16:03.040 --> 00:16:04.530
when you said you wanted to be here?

00:16:04.530 --> 00:16:06.490
A topic that I need to touch on,

00:16:06.490 --> 00:16:08.900
and I wanted you to pay
attention to me very carefully,

00:16:08.900 --> 00:16:11.330
is do not be a gunner.

00:16:11.330 --> 00:16:12.870
Let me define what a gunner is.

00:16:12.870 --> 00:16:16.550
A gunner is a person, a
medical student usually,

00:16:16.550 --> 00:16:19.760
who will do anything to succeed,

00:16:19.760 --> 00:16:22.162
will throw their fellow
students underneath the bus.

00:16:22.162 --> 00:16:23.347
Underneath the bus.

00:16:23.347 --> 00:16:25.910
Will throw their fellow
students under the bus,

00:16:25.910 --> 00:16:30.910
they'll speak out of turn
to make residents look bad,

00:16:31.430 --> 00:16:34.660
they'll tell somebody something
negative about somebody

00:16:34.660 --> 00:16:35.880
in order to get ahead.

00:16:35.880 --> 00:16:37.610
Don't be that person.

00:16:37.610 --> 00:16:39.940
Medicine is a community.

00:16:39.940 --> 00:16:43.600
Build each other up, if you
see a weakness in somebody

00:16:43.600 --> 00:16:44.740
help them fix it.

00:16:44.740 --> 00:16:46.870
And for God's sakes I
see this happen a lot,

00:16:46.870 --> 00:16:49.500
somebody will read some
chapter in a textbook,

00:16:49.500 --> 00:16:51.420
think they're experts on a subject,

00:16:51.420 --> 00:16:53.898
and then start critiquing
residents, attendings,

00:16:53.898 --> 00:16:56.960
lecturers, about something they just read

00:16:56.960 --> 00:16:58.300
and they think they are an expert on,

00:16:58.300 --> 00:17:00.860
when someone that has 20
years of clinical experience

00:17:00.860 --> 00:17:02.258
is telling them otherwise.

00:17:02.258 --> 00:17:04.010
Look, don't get me wrong,

00:17:04.010 --> 00:17:05.150
you can get really knowledgeable

00:17:05.150 --> 00:17:06.480
by reading a textbook,

00:17:06.480 --> 00:17:09.020
but it doesn't replace
real life knowledge.

00:17:09.020 --> 00:17:11.450
Plus, don't make someone feel stupid

00:17:11.450 --> 00:17:12.570
just because you know something

00:17:12.570 --> 00:17:13.920
when you looked at it the day before.

00:17:13.920 --> 00:17:15.940
So don't try and show somebody up.

00:17:15.940 --> 00:17:17.890
That's a gunner, don't be a gunner.

00:17:17.890 --> 00:17:19.770
So during your third year,

00:17:19.770 --> 00:17:21.873
you'll have some time for electives,

00:17:21.873 --> 00:17:24.730
and going on these different rotations

00:17:24.730 --> 00:17:27.130
where you can sort of figure out

00:17:27.130 --> 00:17:29.420
what career trajectory you want.

00:17:29.420 --> 00:17:31.461
At this point you'll
know your Step 1 score,

00:17:31.461 --> 00:17:35.000
you'll know what that
Step 1 score can get you,

00:17:35.000 --> 00:17:36.130
residency wise.

00:17:36.130 --> 00:17:37.820
This is where it's really important

00:17:37.820 --> 00:17:39.280
that when you go to all these hospitals,

00:17:39.280 --> 00:17:40.300
you go to all these clinical sites,

00:17:40.300 --> 00:17:41.811
is that you leave a good impression.

00:17:41.811 --> 00:17:44.170
You leave the impression
that you're eager to learn,

00:17:44.170 --> 00:17:45.450
that you're eager to get better.

00:17:45.450 --> 00:17:47.840
Some people call these showcase electives

00:17:47.840 --> 00:17:50.990
where if you want to do a
certain residency in a hospital,

00:17:50.990 --> 00:17:52.920
you go do your clinical rotation there

00:17:52.920 --> 00:17:54.230
and you show them your abilities,

00:17:54.230 --> 00:17:56.120
your skills, your ability to lead,

00:17:56.120 --> 00:17:57.760
your ability to do hands-on things.

00:17:57.760 --> 00:18:00.320
That's a good time to make connections,

00:18:00.320 --> 00:18:02.340
that's a good time to
show off your personality,

00:18:02.340 --> 00:18:03.340
that's a good time to see

00:18:03.340 --> 00:18:04.710
if you're a good fit at the program.

00:18:04.710 --> 00:18:06.500
Remember, you're still
learning about yourself,

00:18:06.500 --> 00:18:07.870
even though the program's equally

00:18:07.870 --> 00:18:09.380
learning about you at the same time.

00:18:09.380 --> 00:18:12.120
I did my family medicine rotation

00:18:12.120 --> 00:18:13.260
and I fell in love.

00:18:13.260 --> 00:18:15.700
I said that, you know, I love the ability

00:18:15.700 --> 00:18:17.100
to influence someone's life

00:18:17.100 --> 00:18:19.190
outside of just talking
about their disease.

00:18:19.190 --> 00:18:20.280
I fell in love with the field,

00:18:20.280 --> 00:18:21.780
I fell in love with my ability

00:18:21.780 --> 00:18:23.700
to communicate with patients,

00:18:23.700 --> 00:18:26.040
to grow older alongside with my patients.

00:18:26.040 --> 00:18:29.260
I made a lot of the
electives that I had planned

00:18:29.260 --> 00:18:30.730
to be family medicine.

00:18:30.730 --> 00:18:32.976
And I did those electives in

00:18:32.976 --> 00:18:34.810
a bunch of different hospitals,

00:18:34.810 --> 00:18:36.932
just so I could see how
family medicine is practiced

00:18:36.932 --> 00:18:39.051
in different areas in New York.

00:18:39.051 --> 00:18:41.470
I even did a rotation in Florida,

00:18:41.470 --> 00:18:43.486
and that's when time comes

00:18:43.486 --> 00:18:47.490
and you have to do your
showcase electives,

00:18:47.490 --> 00:18:49.160
where you show your abilities

00:18:49.160 --> 00:18:50.820
to the programs for that month,

00:18:50.820 --> 00:18:54.080
that you essentially
wanna do residency in,

00:18:54.080 --> 00:18:56.500
and the last part of that would be

00:18:56.500 --> 00:18:58.190
filling out the Match paperwork.

00:18:58.190 --> 00:18:59.910
So I went into the Match,

00:18:59.910 --> 00:19:01.650
ranked some of these programs,

00:19:01.650 --> 00:19:04.340
it's a time where you have
to do some soul searching,

00:19:04.340 --> 00:19:05.720
you have to get ready for your interviews

00:19:05.720 --> 00:19:06.800
all over again.

00:19:06.800 --> 00:19:08.560
Get used to talking about yourself,

00:19:08.560 --> 00:19:10.670
get used to talking about
your accomplishments again,

00:19:10.670 --> 00:19:12.200
like I said before, verbalize.

00:19:12.200 --> 00:19:14.030
So I went on these interviews,

00:19:14.030 --> 00:19:15.380
went to interviews in Florida,

00:19:15.380 --> 00:19:17.310
went to interviews in
New York, New Jersey.

00:19:17.310 --> 00:19:19.510
Picked Overlook, couldn't be happier now,

00:19:19.510 --> 00:19:20.724
looking back four years later.

00:19:20.724 --> 00:19:22.570
Best choice I made.

00:19:22.570 --> 00:19:24.460
I was so happy with my training there.

00:19:24.460 --> 00:19:26.640
I'd love to tell you
more about my residency

00:19:26.640 --> 00:19:28.670
and my social media and
how all that came together

00:19:28.670 --> 00:19:30.540
but I feel like that
warrants it's own video.

00:19:30.540 --> 00:19:31.770
If you're curious to see that,

00:19:31.770 --> 00:19:33.160
leave a comment down below.

00:19:33.160 --> 00:19:35.384
As you can tell, I'm very
active in the comments section.

00:19:35.384 --> 00:19:39.323
I try to answer all serious questions,

00:19:39.323 --> 00:19:40.840
I know I get a lot of you jokers out there

00:19:40.840 --> 00:19:42.090
but I even answer those, so.

00:19:42.090 --> 00:19:45.292
I really hope that you
enjoyed this long video,

00:19:45.292 --> 00:19:48.050
I know it's longer than
most of my other videos,

00:19:48.050 --> 00:19:51.360
but it was an opportunity
for me to tell you my story.

00:19:51.360 --> 00:19:52.770
So thank you for listening,

00:19:52.770 --> 00:19:55.150
hope you got some great tips and points

00:19:55.150 --> 00:19:57.120
from this video that you
can use in your own life

00:19:57.120 --> 00:20:00.240
and do even a better job
in med school than I did.

00:20:00.240 --> 00:20:03.243
So, stay happy, stay
healthy, see ya next week.

00:20:03.243 --> 00:20:04.634
(static)
Med school

00:20:04.634 --> 00:20:05.983
is not for the weak.

